(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-02"] = {"2.1":{"s":"A solution is a uniform mixture of two or more substances. The one present in larger amount is the solvent; the others are solutes. Air, sea water and alloys are solutions. This chapter covers how much dissolves, vapour pressure of solutions, and colligative properties."},"2.2":{"s":"Solute and solvent can each be a solid, a liquid or a gas, so there are nine types of solution. Oxygen in water is gas in liquid. Alcohol in water is liquid in liquid. Sugar in water is solid in liquid. Hydrogen in palladium is gas in solid, and brass is solid in solid."},"2.3":{"s":"As you add solute, it dissolves quickly at first and then more slowly. Finally no more dissolves: the solution is saturated, and dissolving and crystallising happen at the same rate. Whether a solid dissolves depends on how strong the solute-solvent attractions are compared with those it must break."},"2.4":{"s":"Solubility is the amount of solute in a saturated solution at a given temperature, usually in mol L⁻¹. 'Like dissolves like': polar solutes dissolve in polar solvents. Temperature changes solubility. Gases become less soluble on heating. Henry's law: a gas's solubility is proportional to its pressure above the liquid, which is why soda fizzes when opened.","f":[{"x":"S = K_H*P","n":"Henry's law: solubility of a gas (mol/L) is proportional to its pressure P (bar); K_H in mol L^-1 bar^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.4, p.30"}],"w":["NH₃ and CO₂ dissolve more than Henry's law predicts because they react with water.","Gases dissolve LESS in hot water; many solids dissolve more."]},"2.5":{"s":"Over a mixture of two volatile liquids, each one adds its own vapour pressure. Raoult's law: the partial vapour pressure of each component equals its mole fraction times the vapour pressure of the pure liquid. Ideal solutions obey it exactly. Real solutions can show positive or negative deviations, depending on whether the new attractions are weaker or stronger.","f":[{"x":"P_1 = x_1*P_1^0","n":"Raoult's law: partial vapour pressure of each volatile component; x = mole fraction, P^0 = pure vapour pressure.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.5, p.31"},{"x":"P = (P_2^0 - P_1^0)*x_2 + P_1^0","n":"Total vapour pressure of an ideal binary solution: a straight line in x_2.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.5, p.31"}],"w":["Positive deviation: weaker attractions in the mixture (ethanol + acetone). Negative deviation: stronger (chloroform + acetone)."]},"2.6":{"s":"Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles, not on what they are. There are four: relative lowering of vapour pressure, boiling point elevation, freezing point depression and osmotic pressure. Measuring any of them lets you find the molar mass of the solute.","w":["Colligative properties depend on the NUMBER of solute particles, not on what they are."]},"2.7":{"s":"A non-volatile solute takes up part of the surface, so fewer solvent molecules escape and the vapour pressure falls. The relative lowering of vapour pressure, (P° − P)/P°, equals the mole fraction of the solute.","f":[{"x":"(P_1^0 - P_1)/P_1^0 = x_2","n":"Relative lowering of vapour pressure = mole fraction of the nonvolatile solute.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.7, p.34"},{"x":"(P_1^0 - P_1)/P_1^0 = (W_2*M_1)/(M_2*W_1)","n":"Dilute solution; W = mass, M = molar mass (1 = solvent, 2 = solute).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.7, p.35"}]},"2.8":{"s":"A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals the outside pressure. A solute lowers the vapour pressure, so a higher temperature is needed: the boiling point rises. ΔTb = Kb × m, where m is the molality and Kb is the ebullioscopic constant of the solvent.","f":[{"x":"Delta T_b = K_b*m","n":"Boiling point elevation; m = molality, K_b = ebullioscopic constant (K kg mol^-1).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.8, p.36"},{"x":"M_2 = 1000*K_b*W_2/(Delta T_b*W_1)","n":"Molar mass of solute; W_1 = mass of solvent in grams.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.8, p.37"}],"w":["Use molality (per kg of solvent), not molarity: it does not change with temperature.","A temperature difference is the same in K and in °C."]},"2.9":{"s":"A solution freezes at a lower temperature than the pure solvent. ΔTf = Kf × m, where Kf is the cryoscopic constant. This is why salt melts ice on roads and antifreeze protects car radiators.","f":[{"x":"Delta T_f = K_f*m","n":"Freezing point depression; K_f = cryoscopic constant.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.9, p.38"},{"x":"M_2 = 1000*K_f*W_2/(Delta T_f*W_1)","n":"Molar mass of solute from freezing point depression.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.9, p.38"}]},"2.10":{"s":"Osmosis is the flow of solvent through a semipermeable membrane from a dilute solution into a concentrated one. Osmotic pressure is the pressure needed to stop it: π = CRT, like the gas equation. Isotonic solutions have equal osmotic pressure. Applying more pressure than π reverses the flow (reverse osmosis), which is used to purify water.","f":[{"x":"pi = M*R*T","n":"Osmotic pressure; M = molarity, R = 0.08206 L atm K^-1 mol^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.10, p.40"},{"x":"M_2 = W_2*R*T/(pi*V)","n":"Molar mass of solute; V in litres.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.10, p.40"}],"w":["Isotonic solutions have equal osmotic pressure; solvent flows from hypotonic to hypertonic.","Osmotic pressure is the best method for large molecules (proteins, polymers): the effect is large."]},"2.11":{"s":"Electrolytes split into ions, so they give more particles than their formula suggests and larger colligative effects. The van't Hoff factor i is the observed effect divided by the expected one: about 2 for NaCl, about 3 for CaCl₂. Each formula above is multiplied by i.","f":[{"x":"i = (Delta T_f)/(Delta T_f)_0","n":"van't Hoff factor: observed colligative property over that expected for no dissociation; also = M_theoretical/M_observed.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.11, p.42"},{"x":"Delta T_f = i*K_f*m","n":"Electrolytes: each colligative formula is multiplied by i (likewise Delta T_b, pi = i M R T, Delta P).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.11, p.42"},{"x":"alpha = (i - 1)/(n - 1)","n":"Degree of dissociation; n = ions from one formula unit.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.11, p.43"}],"w":["i ≈ 2 for NaCl and KCl, ≈ 3 for CaCl₂ and Na₂SO₄ in very dilute solution; i < 1 means association.","Abnormal (low) molar mass from colligative data means the solute has dissociated."]}};
